You can find in the following directories several examples that were implemented with
XFaceMaker. The examples cover a wide range of difficulty: some are very simple, others
are sophisticated. You are welcome to look for ideas in these directories.

To build these examples, set the  NSLHOME environment variable to the
directory where the products have been installed, add $NSLHOME/bin to your PATH
variable so you can access xfm. If your system supports shared libraries, add
$NSLHOME/lib to your LD_LIBRARY_PATH or LIBPATH variable.
In directory $NSLHOME/demos/xfm, execute the following commands

	make Makefile
	make Makefiles

This builds the Makefile for each directory.

You are now ready to build the applications you want to run.
cd to the directory whose application you want to build, and execute the command
	make


Directory name		Contents

Adder			A small application that creates an adding machine. It exists
			in 3 versions: 
			adder: interface and application communicate through function calls
			adderAv: Active values are used for interface and application communication
			adderInt: Internationalized version of Adder
Age			A small application that prints the value of a scale.
			Example of defining and using active values to share data between
			the application and the interface.
Alloc			An application to monitor memory allocation for graphical rectangles.
			You can specify the size of the rectangle and click Alloc, or allocate
			data randomly. You can select an allocated rectangle and free it,
			pack the allocated memory, etc.
			This example uses active values for application to interface communication.
Bounce			Game with a bouncing bitmap. When the bitmap reaches the bottom line,
			it bounces if it hit the slider. Otherwise, you have lost, the
			bitmap stops. You can adjust speed with the 'dx' and 'dy' scrollbars
			and the speed with the 'interval' scrollbar. 
			This example uses active values for application to interface communication.
CapXfm			Illustrates the use of FmAddWidgetClass to add widget classes
			to XFaceMaker without using the FACE extensions. 
			See the README in the directory for further detail.
Classes			Examples of widget classes built with XFaceMaker. Some of these
			are mentionned in the User Guide. The classes are: AlertLabel, NumField
			and TextValidate. The directory includes small applications that use
			these classes, and an XFaceMaker executable that includes the classes but
			does not define a class icon for the classes in the toolkit.
Color			Example of an interface made with a group, where the application 
			instantiates the group dynamically.
Cross			Simple example of an interface built with XFaceMaker, where the expose
			callback in a drawing area calls an application function, passing it
			parameters.
CxxClass		Example of C++ class generation. This example  is commented in 
			the XFaceMaker User Guide, in the chapter on C++ class generation.
			You have to run XFaceMaker to generate the C++ class files from the
			Counter.fm file.
DragDrop		Example of defining and using Drag and Drop with active values.
			A drag source and a drop site are defined on two widgets, along 
			with icons that change with the type of data dragged.
Find			Example of a graphic interface implementation for the Unix command
			'find' in which active 	values are used to share data between the 
			application and the interface.
Interfaces		Several interface files built with XFaceMaker. To view them, launch
			XFaceMaker and load them. One of them, Articles, is a good illustration
			of how to do attachments in a Form.
Makeit			Example of a graphic interface implementation for the 'make' command
			in which an active value is used to share data between the application 
			and the interface.
MultiDisplay		Example of displaying an interface on several displays. This shows how
			to open several displays, modify the mainloop to handle events from
			any display. This shows how to use the function FmSetToplevel.
NewAvs			This example shows how to use per-widget and per-call active values.
			See the README in the directory for further detail.
NewXfm			Simple skeleton main program you could use to build a new XFaceMaker
			executable with a new widget class, without using the XFaceMaker extensions capability.
Pulldown		Simple example of a menubar and some pulldown menus built with XFaceMaker.
Skeleton		An example with two widgets, an applicationShell and an XmLabel.
			Use this only to identify problems you may encounter when building
			applications. The executable just shows a Hello world label.
World			Example of an animation. This is a Motif application example that was
			adapted for use with XFaceMaker. This application illustrates the use
			or work procedures in the main loop.

